# ============================================================================
#  tool/live_checks/.env.example  —  TEMPLATE, not a real credential file.
#
#  HOW TO USE
#    1. Copy this file to            tool/live_checks/.env
#       (that exact path; it is already git-ignored — see the note below).
#    2. Paste your project URL and your PUBLISHABLE (legacy name: "anon") key.
#    3. Run:  node tool/live_checks/run_all.js
#
#  WHERE THE TWO VALUES COME FROM
#    Supabase dashboard -> your project -> Project Settings -> "API Keys"
#    (or the Connect / "API" side panel). Copy the key labelled
#    `anon` / `publishable key`, and the Project URL.
#
#  ============================================================================
#  HARD SECURITY RULES
#    * NEVER commit the real .env. `tool/live_checks/.gitignore` and the repo
#      root .gitignore both exclude `.env` / `.env.*`; the negation for this
#      template lives in tool/live_checks/.gitignore so that only *.example*
#      is tracked. Double-check with:
#          git check-ignore -v tool/live_checks/.env   (must report a match)
#          git status --ignored tool/live_checks        (must list .env)
#      If .env ever shows up as "new file" in git status, STOP and fix the
#      ignore rule before committing.
#    * ONLY the PUBLISHABLE / anon key belongs here. It is a PUBLIC, rate-
#      limited browser key: everything it can reach is already filtered by RLS,
#      which is exactly what these scripts are testing.
#    * NEVER put a `service_role` key, a `secret`/server key, a database
#      password, a connection string, or a `postgres://` URL in this file or in
#      your shell for these scripts. A service-role key BYPASSES RLS entirely,
#      so every assertion in rls_matrix.js would "pass" while proving nothing,
#      and its throwaway writes could corrupt real data. The toolkit redacts
#      credential-looking values (lib/config.js) but it cannot un-leak a key
#      that was pasted on purpose.
#    * The ONE account-password slot allowed here is `SUPABASE_ADMIN_PASSWORD`
#      (see the pair section at the bottom): it is a normal end-user login
#      secret, it can only do what that account's RLS roles allow, and the
#      toolkit prints it as presence+length only. Everything above still
#      applies verbatim to service_role/secret keys and database passwords —
#      those have no slot in this toolkit at all.
#    * The optional *_TOKEN variables are short-lived ACCESS tokens from a
#      signed-in browser session (DevTools -> Network -> any request -> the
#      `Authorization: Bearer ...` value, or Supabase-JS `session.access_token`).
#      They expire in about an hour; treat them as secrets and never commit one.
# ============================================================================

# --- REQUIRED --------------------------------------------------------------
# Project URL, no trailing slash.
SUPABASE_URL=https://<your-project-ref>.supabase.co

# The PUBLISHABLE / anon key ONLY. Never the service_role or secret key.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<publishable-anon-key>
# Equivalent accepted aliases (first non-empty wins; see lib/config.js):
# SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=<publishable-anon-key>

# --- OPTIONAL: unlocks the staff / reader planes -----------------------------
# Without these the affected checks report SKIP, never a fake PASS.
# See tool/live_checks/README.md for exactly which planes stay skipped.

# Access token of an account that already has a profiles row with
# role='super_admin' AND a profile_sites row (admin/editor) on india_tech.
# Created by the SQL operator path: supabase/operator/010_bootstrap_first_admin.sql
# SUPABASE_ADMIN_TOKEN=<access-token-of-the-admin-account>

# Access token of an account with NO profile_sites row (a plain reader), used
# to prove signing in grants nothing. If absent, rls_matrix.js tries an
# ephemeral throwaway signup unless you disable it below.
# SUPABASE_READER_TOKEN=<access-token-of-a-no-membership-account>

# Access token of an account that is admin/editor of india_tech ONLY (never
# super_admin). Needed for the strict cross-site isolation probe (F13).
# SUPABASE_SITE_ADMIN_TOKEN=<access-token-of-a-site-only-admin>

# Slug of a SECOND, temporary site used to prove multi-site isolation.
# MUST start with `zzz-check` — anything else (e.g. a production sibling site)
# is hard-rejected by lib/config.js before a request is ever sent.
# CHECK_SECOND_SITE_SLUG=zzz-check-tmp

# Set to 0 to forbid the reader-plane fallback signup (recommended if you do
# not want the suite creating a throwaway auth user).
# ALLOW_THROWAWAY_SIGNUP=1

# --- OPTIONAL: the admin credential PAIR (real sign-in, not a token) ---------
# Only needed to drive an actual email/password sign-in against the project
# (tool/live_checks/admin_flow_mock.js runs the identical flow against a local
# mock and needs NONE of this).
#
# THE RULES THAT APPLY TO THESE TWO LINES:
#   * Both are read from the real environment first, then from THIS git-ignored
#     file only. There is no other intake: not a command-line argument, not a
#     --dart-define, not a source file, not a test fixture, not a screenshot.
#   * The password is never printed. Every report shows `present (length N,
#     sha256:<first8>)` and nothing else (lib/config.js identitySummary /
#     credentialPresence). It is also pushed into cfg.secrets so redact() masks
#     it even if Supabase echoes it back inside an error body.
#   * It is never written to disk by the toolkit and never copied into a report.
#   * tool/build_production.ps1 selects its inputs field-by-field (url / anon
#     key / GA id / verification token), so adding these lines can NOT leak a
#     password into a Flutter Web bundle or a build argument.
#
# The exact email of the operator-created admin account, i.e. the address you
# typed into Authentication -> Users -> "Add user" and then promoted with
# supabase/operator/010_bootstrap_first_admin.sql.
# SUPABASE_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# accepted alias: ADMIN_EMAIL
#
# That account's password. Type/paste it yourself; this repository must never
# contain a real value here (only the commented line above).
# SUPABASE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# accepted alias: ADMIN_PASSWORD

